      { "text": "Pulmonary embolism from immobility on the dive boat; treatment is anticoagulation.", "explanation": "Incorrect. The classic post-dive presentation with joint pain ('the bends') and chest discomfort ('the chokes') is decompression sickness, not PE. This would be the correct answer if he had unilateral leg swelling with imaging-confirmed PE." },
      { "text": "Decompression sickness — nitrogen dissolved at depth comes out of solution as gas bubbles in tissues during rapid ascent, producing joint pain ('bends') and pulmonary symptoms ('chokes'); treatment is hyperbaric oxygen therapy in a recompression chamber.", "explanation": "Correct. The lecturer described decompression sickness as a form of gas embolism caused by sudden decrease in atmospheric pressure during rapid ascent. At depth, increased gas (especially nitrogen) dissolves in blood and tissues; rapid ascent causes the gas to come out of solution as bubbles, producing pain in skeletal muscles and around joints (the bends) and pulmonary symptoms (the chokes). Chronic disease (caisson disease) features ischemic necrosis of the femoral heads. Treatment is hyperbaric oxygen, which forces the gas back into solution while patients breathe high-FiO2." },
      { "text": "Inferior wall myocardial infarction from atherosclerotic plaque rupture; treatment is percutaneous coronary intervention.", "explanation": "Incorrect. The patient is too young and the dive context is more consistent with decompression sickness. This would be the correct answer in an older patient with risk factors and ECG changes consistent with inferior STEMI." },
      { "text": "Anaphylaxis from a marine toxin; treatment is epinephrine.", "explanation": "Incorrect. Anaphylaxis would feature urticaria, airway edema, and hypotension rather than joint pain. This would be the correct answer if he were stung by jellyfish and developed wheezing and angioedema." }
